<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">County Palatine of Durham</h1><p class="st-meta">1075 CE – 1836 CE (approx.) · capital Durham</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The palatinate where the bishops of Durham ruled as prince-bishops for more than seven centuries, guarding England's northern frontier.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-england">Durham</a> inherited the lands and prestige of the community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert">Saint Cuthbert</a>, whose shrine moved to Durham in 995. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walcher-bishop-of-durham">Walcher of Lorraine</a> bishop in 1071 and gave him the earldom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Northumbria</a> in 1075, joining spiritual and secular power on the Scottish border.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> had their own courts, chancery, mint, sheriff and army, and royal writs did not normally run between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">Tyne</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tees">Tees</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-castle">Durham Castle</a>, begun in 1072, and the cathedral, begun in 1093, expressed their power. Bishops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antony-bek">Antony Bek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-langley">Thomas Langley</a> were leading royal servants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> and lead mining added to their wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Tudors curbed the palatinate's independence in 1536. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">University of Durham</a> was founded in 1832 with part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' endowment, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-england">Durham</a> (County Palatine) Act of 1836 transferred the remaining secular powers to the crown.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-palatine-of-durham.jpg" alt="County Palatine of Durham" width="320" height="324"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:County_Durham_Wards_-_definition_B.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1075 CE – 1836 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Durham</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walcher, Antony Bek, Thomas Langley</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity; Church of England after 1534</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle English, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Bishopric of Durham, Palatinate of Durham</dd></dl></aside></div>
